  "infoWhyStrongHold": "Why it's here: a firmer squeeze used in the erection support plan from Level 3.",
  "infoWhyStandingHolds": "Why it's here: leaks often happen upright, so holds move to standing and walking as you progress.",
  "infoWhyBridge": "Why it's here: pairs the pelvic floor squeeze with the hip muscles that support it.",
  "infoWhySquat": "Why it's here: practises relaxing on the way down and squeezing on the way up, the pattern of everyday movement.",
  "infoWhySitToStand": "Why it's here: standing up is a common moment for leaks, so you practise the squeeze at exactly that moment.",
  "infoWhyKnack": "Why it's here: squeezing just before a cough, sneeze or lift trains control in the moments that cause leaks.",
  "infoWhyPillowSqueeze": "Why it's here: the inner thigh muscles work together with the pelvic floor.",
  "infoWhyBreathing": "Why it's here: teaches the pelvic floor to let go fully between squeezes.",
  "infoWhyReverseKegel": "Why it's here: lengthens and relaxes the muscles, useful when they feel tight or squeezing is uncomfortable.",
  "infoWhyStretch": "Why it's here: a gentle stretch that helps the pelvic floor relax.",
  "infoWhyAfterDribble": "Why it's here: prostate charities recommend it to clear the last drops that stay in the urethra after you finish.",
  "infoWhyUrgeControl": "Why it's here: bladder guidelines include these steps; staying calm and still gives a sudden urge time to fade.",
  "infoWhyWalking": "Why it's here: regular aerobic activity supports heart and blood vessel health.",

  "infoPelvicTitle": "Pelvic floor session",
  "infoPelvicBody": "Guided slow holds and quick squeezes. Squeeze and lift as if stopping yourself passing wind, then let go fully before the next one.",
